messages:
maya-docs: Heads-up from onboarding review: the quickstart sample app currently includes a hardcoded token in the example ("const TOKEN = 'REPLACE_ME'"). New users are copy/pasting that into public repos or Gists and then opening support tickets when the token gets used elsewhere. Also the README shows a naive "auto-rotate" loop that polls the horizon and writes new keys to disk — not great.

ran-support: Confirmed. Two customers reported leaked tokens after pasting the sample to GitHub to share with teammates. Support had to revoke keys and hand-hold rotation. This is adding churn for SRE and trust issues for customers.

eli-sdk: We should provide an idiomatic rotation example using the SDK's refresh helper and env vars. Proposal: replace hardcoded tokens with a short snippet showing ENV usage plus a tiny refresh function: 
```js
// Node (recommended): keep secrets out of source
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
// SDK will auto-refresh if you wire in a token provider
client.setTokenProvider(async () => {
  // fetch ephemeral token from your backend which holds long-lived creds
  const r = await fetch(`${process.env.AUTH_ORIGIN}/session-token`);
  return (await r.json()).shortLivedToken;
});
```
This demonstrates delegated rotation (client never persists the long-lived secret).

zoe-security: +1. Add a short best-practices blurb: "Never check tokens into VCS. Use short-lived tokens or a refresh flow; store long-lived secrets in a KMS-backed vault (AWS Secrets Manager, GCP Secret Manager) and mint ephemeral tokens for clients." Also recommend adding an example server snippet that mints ephemeral tokens using KMS.

maya-docs: Good. We'll also remove the disk-write rotation example and replace with a small server + client pattern. Also include a one-line command to scan for accidental tokens in repos (`git grep -n "REPLACE_ME"` and `trufflehog` note) in the onboarding docs.

eli-sdk: Another change: add `idempotency-key` guidance when showing retry examples. We currently show a loop that retries POSTs without idempotency; if users copy that into production they might create duplicate resources. Suggest adding `Idempotency-Key: uuidv4()` in examples and show how SDK helper does it automatically: `client.generate({..., idempotencyKey: uuid})`.

zoe-security: Also add a short note about credential revocation: when rotating, revoke old keys and use short TTLs for ephemeral tokens. We should include a sample server endpoint for rotation that returns a 5m token and logs issuance events to audit logs.

ran-support: Can we also add a small troubleshooting FAQ bullet: "I pasted sample and now my token is leaked" with steps: revoke, rotate, search repos, notify team. That'll reduce support noise.

ci-bot: Created issue https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/issues/1720 assigned to @maya-docs for: (1) remove hardcoded token snippet, (2) add SDK tokenProvider pattern example, (3) add server minting example (Node/Python), (4) add idempotency-key examples and FAQ. Also adding a docs-lint rule to flag likely hardcoded tokens and to require idempotency guidance near retry examples.

maya-docs: I'll open the PR with minimal runnable examples and a changelog entry "docs: replace hardcoded token example with secure tokenProvider pattern; add idempotency and rotation guidance". Will also update the CLI quickstart templates to use the environment-based pattern instead of inline tokens.

eli-sdk: I'll add two small unit tests to SDK examples: one that asserts snippets do not contain literal token patterns and one that verifies example shows idempotency header usage.

zoe-security: Thanks  this should cut down on leaked-key tickets and make onboarding safer.

maya-docs: :thumbsup:
